      "text": "To solve the sum \\(\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty \\frac{1}{x^{2^n} - x^{-2^n}}\\), we first need to simplify the general term of the sum.\n\nThe general term is:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{x^{2^n} - x^{-2^n}}.\n\\]\nWe can rewrite this as:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{x^{2^n} - \\frac{1}{x^{2^n}}} = \\frac{1}{\\frac{x^{2^{n+1}} - 1}{x^{2^n}}} = \\frac{x^{2^n}}{x^{2^{n+1}} - 1}.\n\\]\nSo the sum becomes:\n\\[\n\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty \\frac{x^{2^n}}{x^{2^{n+1}} - 1}.\n\\]\nWe can use the method of telescoping sums to evaluate this sum. Notice that:\n\\[\n\\frac{x^{2^n}}{x^{2^{n+1}} - 1} = \\frac{x^{2^n}}{(x^{2^n})^2 - 1} = \\frac{x^{2^n}}{(x^{2^n} - 1)(x^{2^n} + 1)}.\n\\]\nFor \\(x > 1\\), we can use partial fraction decomposition:\n\\[\n\\frac{x^{2^n}}{(x^{2^n} - 1)(x^{2^n} + 1)} = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\frac{1}{x^{2^n} - 1} - \\frac{1}{x^{2^n} + 1} \\right).\n\\]\nSo the sum becomes:\n\\[\n\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty \\frac{x^{2^n}}{(x^{2^n} - 1)(x^{2^n} + 1)} = \\frac{1}{2} \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{1}{x^{2^n} - 1} - \\frac{1}{x^{2^n} + 1} \\right).\n\\]\nThis sum is telescoping, and it simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\frac{1}{x^0 - 1} - \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{1}{x^{2^n} + 1} \\right) = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\frac{1}{0 - 1} - \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{1}{x^{2^n} + 1} \\right) = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( -1 - 0 \\right) = \\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}.\n\\]\nTo confirm the result, we can use Python to compute the partial sum for a large number of terms and check its convergence to \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\).\n```python\n# Function to compute the partial sum of the series\r\ndef partial_sum(x, terms=100):\r\n    return sum(1 / (x**(2**n) - x**(-2**n)) for n in range(terms))\r\n\r\n# Compute the partial sum for a reasonable number of terms\r\npartial_sum_value = partial_sum(2)  # Let's use x = 2 as an example\r\nprint(partial_sum_value)\n```",
